  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data explored:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` has 14,074 requests. Status codes are mostly 200 (12,832), then 404 (801) and 503 (441). Traffic comes mainly from CN, IN, and US. The timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so part of the data is later than today (2026-10-12).\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-cfb7234f Web logs overview\" (id `eff-cfb7234f-web-logs-overview-6cef4be926bc`) has six panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Requests over time (line, by `@timestamp`)\n- Responses by status code (pie, by `response.keyword`)\n- Top destination countries (bar, by `geo.dest`)\n- Total bytes transferred (metric, sum of `bytes`)\n- 5xx responses (ES|QL metric)\n\nI set the dashboard time range to 2026-10-01 through 2026-12-31 so that it covers all the sample data.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-cfb7234f 5xx spike\" (id `941329e7-2e93-4693-b7ef-bacae763bbb4`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every 1 minute, counts documents whose `response.keyword` is between 500 and 599 over the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- The rule will probably never fire on this sample data. Every 5xx response is a 503, there are only 441 in total, and no 5-minute bucket has more than 2. The threshold is what you specified, so I left it as is.\n- The rule's `size` is 100, which is the number of hits the rule returns for its action context. It does not change the count threshold.\n- The rule is enabled and its status is \"pending\" until its first run. Alerting health reports OK, with an encryption key and a secure connection.",
